  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "text": "Ten million times slower. One second now lasts about one hundred sixteen days. A hundred million times slower, and one second lasts more than three years. At this speed, a flash of light would take almost two seconds to cross the room. Now we're close. A billion times slower. One second now lasts about thirty-two years. Now the light itself is slow. Light moves about thirty centimeters in a billionth of a second. So at this speed, it crawls thirty centimeters in one second. That's a slow crawl. Someone flicks on a flashlight. A glowing front of light leaves the lens, and spreads across the room. Behind it, the table is lit. Ahead of it, the table is still dark. The cup lights up on one side first, and its shadow arrives last. The drop of water above the cup hangs there, frozen. It takes about seventeen seconds for the light to reach the far wall. And in the corner, a computer chip is about to start ticking.",
